/// \defgroup grp_w1023b_apt_math_date_natives Apt Math and Date natives, and AptValue string assignment
/// \ingroup grp_gui_apt
/// \brief The native code behind the ActionScript `Math` and `Date` objects
///        in Apt movies, and the call that stores text in a string value.
///
/// Apt movies run ActionScript bytecode in the interpreter described on
/// \ref sub_gui_apt_runtime. Some of what a script can call is not bytecode
/// but native C++ that the interpreter reaches through native-function
/// values. This group covers two such sets and one supporting call:
/// - `Math`: seventeen of the eighteen member functions of the Math object
///   (all but `random`). They share one calling contract, written once on the
///   page of the file AptMathCallbacksBFME2.cpp, which also lists every
///   name the Math lookup accepts.
/// - `Date`: the class AptDate (its two clock readings, its 37 member names
///   and their quirks) and three of its functions: AptDate::setDates,
///   AptDate::dateGetNumDaysInMonth and AptDate::CleanNativeFunctions.
/// - AptValue::SetString, which the interpreter and the native objects use to
///   put text into a string value, and the class AptValue it belongs to.
///
/// For Apt UI authors the group fixes which Math and Date methods exist, how
/// their arguments are checked and converted, and where they depart from a
/// general ActionScript runtime. For a remaster it shows what these natives
/// depend on: the C runtime and x87 instructions for Math, a host-supplied
/// clock function for Date, and a single global interpreter object.
///
/// \see \ref sub_gui_apt, \ref sub_gui_apt_modding, \ref sub_gui_apt_remastering


/// \file AptMathCallbacksBFME2.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_apt_math_date_natives
/// \brief The native `Math` methods of the Apt ActionScript interpreter, and
///        the calling contract they all share.
///
/// Apt movies are scripted in ActionScript bytecode (\ref sub_gui_apt_runtime).
/// A call such as `Math.floor(x)` in a movie ends in native code. The member
/// lookup of the Math object, AptMathObj::objectMemberLookup, turns a member
/// name into a native-function value that holds the address of one of the
/// callbacks documented in this file, and the interpreter calls such a value
/// through AptActionInterpreter::callFunction. How the name `Math` is resolved
/// to the object that owns this lookup was not traced for this page; the page
/// describes the lookup step and the call step only. All of these callbacks
/// have the same shape, so the rules are written down once here and each
/// function below adds only what is specific to it.
///
/// \par The names that exist
/// The lookup accepts exactly these 18 member names. It matches them with a
/// case-sensitive perfect-hash table, so the table is the complete list: no
/// other member of `Math` is served by this lookup.
///
/// | ActionScript name | Callback | Arguments used | Result kind |
/// |---|---|---|---|
/// | `Math.sin` | aptMathSin | 1 | float |
/// | `Math.cos` | aptMathCos | 1 | float |
/// | `Math.tan` | aptMathTan | 1 | float |
/// | `Math.asin` | aptMathAsin | 1 | float |
/// | `Math.acos` | aptMathAcos | 1 | float |
/// | `Math.atan` | aptMathAtan | 1 | float |
/// | `Math.atan2` | aptMathAtan2 | 2 (y, then x) | float |
/// | `Math.exp` | aptMathExp | 1 | float |
/// | `Math.log` | aptMathLog | 1 | float |
/// | `Math.pow` | aptMathPow | 2 (base, then exponent) | float |
/// | `Math.sqrt` | aptMathSqrt | 1 | float |
/// | `Math.floor` | aptMathFloor | 1 | float |
/// | `Math.ceil` | aptMathCeil | 1 | float |
/// | `Math.round` | aptMathRound | 1 | integer |
/// | `Math.abs` | aptMathAbs | 1 | integer for an integer argument, else float |
/// | `Math.min` | aptMathMin | 2 | float |
/// | `Math.max` | aptMathMax | 2 | float |
/// | `Math.random` | `aptMathRandom` (not covered here; see \ref sub_gui_apt_modding) | 0 | float |
///
/// The lookup serves no numeric constants (there is no `Math.PI` entry in it).
/// Whether the Math object can carry such properties some other way is not
/// established here.
///
/// A name written with the wrong case, for example `Math.Floor`, gets no member
/// from this lookup. The lookup then compares the name case-insensitively against the same 18
/// names. On a hit it writes a line starting `AptMathObj: Incorrect case for`
/// and quoting the offending name to Apt's log hook at level 3, raises an Apt
/// assertion (the retail assertion names the original source file
/// AptMiscObjects.cpp) and, only when Apt's debug-break flag is set, breaks
/// into the debugger. The lookup returns no member either way. Whether the log
/// hook, the assertion hook and the break flag do anything in the shipped game
/// is not established here.
///
/// This page describes only the native lookup step. The interpreter's variable
/// lookup, AptActionInterpreter::getVariable, calls findChild on the object
/// (the first call only when the context was resolved from the name) before
/// and again after the object's member lookup, and runs the
/// undefined-variable callback only after those fallbacks. So "this
/// lookup returns no member" does not by itself mean that the name resolves to
/// nothing. Whether a movie can store members on the Math object, and whether
/// dotted member access takes that path, are not established here.
///
/// Each native-function value is built lazily. The first lookup of a name
/// allocates a small native-function object that stores the callback address,
/// marks it as a garbage-collection root, takes one reference to it and keeps
/// it in a global cache slot. Later lookups of the same name return that
/// object. The same scheme serves the Date members (see AptDate).
///
/// \par Calling contract
/// - Signature: `AptValue *callback(void *self, int argc)`, C calling
///   convention.
/// - AptActionInterpreter::callFunction calls it for a native-function value
///   with the context value that was passed to callFunction (the callback's
///   `self`; which object that is for a `Math.x(...)` call was not traced) and
///   the number of arguments the script passed. After the callback returns, the
///   interpreter pops those arguments off its value stack and pushes the
///   returned value, so a callback never pops anything itself.
/// - The arguments are left on the interpreter's value stack with the first
///   script argument on top. A callback reads them through
///   AptBasePtrStack::At(0) for the first argument, At(1) for the second. The
///   same convention is used when a script function receives its arguments.
/// - The callbacks do not use `self`. They read the stack of the one global
///   interpreter object directly rather than taking an interpreter parameter.
/// - `argc` is checked first. When it is lower than the number of arguments
///   the function needs, the callback returns the shared undefined value and
///   does not touch the stack. Extra arguments are ignored and discarded by
///   the interpreter.
/// - AptBasePtrStack::At asserts that the position lies inside the stack. The
///   assertion goes to Apt's assert hook and is not a clamp, so it cannot
///   repair a bad position. The `argc` check normally makes it unreachable.
///
/// \par How arguments are converted
/// Every numeric argument goes through the same conversion of an Apt value to
/// a 32-bit float, the shared numeric conversion in AptValueToNumberBFME2.cpp:
/// - the undefined value converts to 0;
/// - a string is parsed by the C runtime's `atof`; how that function treats
///   text such as "nan" or "inf" is a property of that runtime and is not
///   established here;
/// - a boolean converts to 1 or 0;
/// - an integer value converts to a float;
/// - a float value is used as it is, so a NaN float produced by an earlier call
///   stays a NaN;
/// - any other kind of value (an object, array, movie clip or function)
///   converts to 1, unless it is the shared undefined value, which converts to 0.
///
/// The conversion is wider than a plain number check. `Math.sqrt("16")` and
/// `Math.abs(true)` work and give 4 and 1, and `Math.floor(someMovieClip)`
/// gives 1.
///
/// \par Results
/// - Callers receive a freshly created, pooled Apt value: a float for every
///   function except the two noted in the table, an integer for
///   aptMathRound and for aptMathAbs with an integer argument.
/// - A float result holds a 32-bit float. The arithmetic runs at x87 or
///   double precision on the converted argument and the result is narrowed to
///   32 bits when it is boxed. Scripts therefore see about seven significant
///   decimal digits, whatever the argument was.
/// - Angles are in radians throughout.
///
/// \par How the C runtime and the FPU are used
/// The BFME 2 source and the ledger rows for these callbacks record the
/// following:
/// - x87 instructions: sin (fsin), cos (fcos), tan (fptan), sqrt (fsqrt) and
///   atan2 (fpatan). min and max select with an x87 comparison, and the float
///   path of abs uses fabs.
/// - pow calls the C runtime's _CIpow entry point. floor and ceil call the C
///   runtime's `floor` and `ceil` through the executable's import thunks for
///   msvcr71.dll.
/// - round truncates to an integer with the `ftol2` helper.
/// - acos, asin, atan, exp and log apply the C library function of that name to
///   the argument widened to a double and box the result as a float. Whether
///   the compiler emitted each as inline x87 code or as a call is not
///   established here.
/// - The callback bodies contain no domain check. `Math.sqrt(-1)`,
///   `Math.log(0)` and `Math.acos(2)` give whatever the instruction or C
///   library function produces, boxed as a float value.
///
/// \par BFME 1
/// Open-BFME-1's reconstruction of BFME 1's Math callbacks reads the stack
/// array directly, with no bounds assertion. BFME 2 goes through
/// AptBasePtrStack::At. Open-BFME-1's abs callback converts its argument to an
/// integer and always returns an integer; BFME 2's tests for an integer value
/// first and handles floats separately (see aptMathAbs). Both observations
/// rest on the BFME 1 donor source and the BFME 2 bodies, read side by side.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Hard-coded: the native lookup serves exactly these 18 names,
///   case-sensitively, and returns no member for any other name; the argument
///   order and the conversion rules above are also fixed in the callbacks. The
///   interpreter then falls back to the object's own children (findChild), and
///   whether a movie can store members on Math is not established here. The
///   lookup serves no constants such as pi; a movie can compute them in its own
///   ActionScript.
/// - Data-driven: which of these functions a movie calls, with which
///   arguments, and what it does with the results are decided by the movie's
///   ActionScript (\ref sub_gui_apt_modding). The functions themselves are
///   fixed in the engine: the arithmetic of `sin`, `pow`, `floor` and the rest
///   runs in the native callbacks above, with the conversions and the
///   single-precision results described there, so changing what one of them
///   computes needs an engine change, not a movie change. Which shipped movies
///   call which Math functions was not examined for this page.
/// - Behaviour differences from a general ActionScript runtime that matter when
///   porting scripts: results are single precision; `Math.round` rounds ties
///   away from zero (2.5 gives 3 and -2.5 gives -3); `Math.min` and
///   `Math.max` always return a float; strings are parsed by the C runtime's
///   `atof` rather than by an ECMAScript-style string-to-number rule, and the
///   results for text that is not a plain number are those of that runtime;
///   objects convert to 1.
/// - Lockstep: these callbacks read no game state; the only thing they read
///   is their arguments on the interpreter's value stack. The `Math.random`
///   function, which is not covered here, uses a separate generator rather
///   than the logic RNG (see \ref sub_gui_apt_modding). The project's Apt
///   modding notes (\ref sub_gui_apt_modding) tie the lockstep risk to UI
///   callbacks that touch logic-side state directly. Whether any shipped movie
///   routes a Math result that way is not established here (\ref
///   page_modding).
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The callbacks depend on the C runtime (pow, floor and ceil are recorded as
///   calls into msvcr71.dll) and on x87 instructions (sin, cos, tan, sqrt,
///   atan2 and the min and max compares), and the rest apply C library
///   functions to a widened double. A port to another compiler, runtime or CPU
///   can change the low bits of the transcendental results. Scripts only
///   notice if they compare results exactly or feed them into logic.
/// - Each native-function object stores its callback address in a 32-bit
///   integer when it is created, so a 64-bit build has to change that
///   constructor and the cache slots.
/// - The callbacks reach the interpreter through one global object. A build
///   that wants more than one interpreter has to change every callback of this
///   kind (the Date callbacks too), not just this file. See
///   \ref page_remastering and \ref sub_gui_apt_remastering for the other UI
///   seams.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Names: the BFME 2 callback names are the ledger's. Open-BFME-1's
/// per-function units supply the algorithms (a donor fact). In that donor tree
/// file names and function names differ in places. For fourteen of the Math
/// callbacks the donor file and the function it defines both carry the BFME 2
/// callback's name. The donor's pow function is also named aptMathPow, but it
/// sits in a file whose name is address-derived. The donor's min and max
/// functions are named aptMin and aptMax, which differ from aptMathMin and
/// aptMathMax. Name equality, here or anywhere in the donor tree, is not
/// treated as identity evidence. The binding
/// of each ActionScript name to its callback is a BFME 2 fact, read from
/// BFME 2's own member lookup. The
/// argument order, the result kinds and the conversion rules are read from the
/// BFME 2 bodies and their callees. The file name's BFME2 suffix only labels
/// this unit; it is not part of any callback name.
///
/// \see aptMathFloor, aptMathRound, aptMathAbs, aptMathMin, aptMathPow


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathFloor(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.floor(x)`: rounds its argument down to a whole number.
///
/// Converts the first argument to a float, calls the C runtime's `floor` on
/// it as a double and boxes the result as a float value. The result is a float
/// kind even though its value is whole, which is the difference from
/// aptMathRound. See the file overview for the shared calling contract and the
/// conversion rules.
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 1 is needed.
/// \return A new float value, or the undefined value when `argc` is below 1.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Algorithm from Open-BFME-1's floor callback (donor). The use of the checked
/// stack accessor, the direct C runtime call and the float result are read from
/// the BFME 2 body.
/// \see aptMathCeil, aptMathRound


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathCeil(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.ceil(x)`: rounds its argument up to a whole number.
///
/// The mirror of aptMathFloor: first argument converted to a float, the C
/// runtime's `ceil` on the widened double, result boxed as a float value.
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 1 is needed.
/// \return A new float value, or the undefined value when `argc` is below 1.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Algorithm from Open-BFME-1's ceil callback (donor); call shape read from the
/// BFME 2 body.
/// \see aptMathFloor, aptMathRound


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathRound(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.round(x)`: rounds to the nearest whole number, ties
///        away from zero, and returns an integer value.
///
/// Unlike the float-returning callbacks it always returns an Apt integer
/// (aptMathAbs does so only for an integer argument), so a script that tests
/// the kind of the result sees an integer.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The first argument is converted to a float. If it is greater than zero the
/// function adds 0.5, otherwise it subtracts 0.5, and then truncates toward
/// zero to a 32-bit integer. So 2.4 gives 2, 2.5 gives 3, -2.5 gives -3 and 0
/// gives 0. The compare against zero uses the x87 compare, so an unordered
/// argument (a NaN float) takes the subtract branch. Large magnitudes that do
/// not fit in 32 bits are not handled.
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 1 is needed.
/// \return A new integer value, or the undefined value when `argc` is not
///         positive.
///
/// \note The tie rule differs from ECMAScript's Math.round, which rounds -2.5
///       to -2. A movie ported from another runtime may need a fix-up for
///       negative halves.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Algorithm from Open-BFME-1's round callback (donor). The integer result and
/// the branch on the sign are read from the BFME 2 body.
/// \see aptMathFloor, aptMathCeil


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathAbs(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.abs(x)`: absolute value, keeping integers as integers.
///
/// Looks at the kind of the first argument without converting it.
/// - If it is an Apt integer value, the callback takes its integer, applies an
///   integer absolute value and returns a new integer value.
/// - For every other argument it converts to a float as usual, takes the
///   floating-point absolute value and returns a float value.
///
/// So `Math.abs(-3)` stays an integer, while `Math.abs("-3")` or
/// `Math.abs(-3.5)` give a float.
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 1 is needed.
/// \return A new integer or float value, or the undefined value when `argc` is
///         below 1.
///
/// \note BFME 1: Open-BFME-1's reconstruction of this callback has only the
///       integer path. The integer-or-float split is something BFME 2 adds,
///       read from the integer test and the separate float factory in the
///       BFME 2 body.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Integer path from Open-BFME-1's abs callback (donor); the type test and the
/// float path are read from the BFME 2 body.
/// \see aptMathRound


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathMin(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.min(a, b)`: the smaller of two arguments, always as a
///        float.
///
/// Converts the first two arguments to floats and compares them with an x87
/// compare. The first argument is selected only when it is strictly smaller
/// than the second. In every other case the second argument is selected: a tie
/// (the values are equal anyway) and an unordered comparison, when either
/// value is a NaN float. The chosen argument is converted again and boxed as a
/// float value, so the result is a float even when both arguments were
/// integers. Only two arguments are looked at, so `Math.min(a, b, c)` ignores
/// `c`.
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 2 are needed.
/// \return A new float value, or the undefined value when `argc` is below 2.
///
/// \warning With a NaN, the answer depends on the argument order:
///          `Math.min(NaN, 3)` gives 3 and `Math.min(3, NaN)` gives NaN.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Open-BFME-1 has two sibling units (its functions are named aptMin and
/// aptMax) that differ from each other only in the comparison; their names are
/// not relied on. Which value is selected is read from the BFME 2 x87 compare
/// and branch sequence.
/// \see aptMathMax


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathMax(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.max(a, b)`: the larger of two arguments, always as a
///        float.
///
/// The counterpart of aptMathMin. The first argument is selected only when it
/// is strictly greater than the second. A tie or an unordered comparison (a
/// NaN float) selects the second argument. The selected argument is converted
/// again and boxed as a float value. Arguments beyond the second are ignored.
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 2 are needed.
/// \return A new float value, or the undefined value when `argc` is below 2.
///
/// \warning With a NaN, the answer depends on the argument order, as for
///          aptMathMin.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Open-BFME-1 has two sibling units (its functions are named aptMin and
/// aptMax) that differ from each other only in the comparison; their names are
/// not relied on. Which value is selected, including the unordered case, is
/// read from the BFME 2 x87 compare and branch sequence.
/// \see aptMathMin


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathSin(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.sin(x)`: sine of an angle in radians, as a float.
///
/// First argument converted to a float and passed through the x87 sine
/// instruction (fsin). The result is narrowed to a 32-bit float when boxed.
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 1 is needed.
/// \return A new float value, or the undefined value when `argc` is below 1.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Algorithm from Open-BFME-1's sin callback (donor); the checked accessor and
/// the FPU instruction are read from the BFME 2 body.
/// \see aptMathCos, aptMathTan


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathCos(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.cos(x)`: cosine of an angle in radians, as a float.
///
/// As aptMathSin, with the cosine instruction.
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 1 is needed.
/// \return A new float value, or the undefined value when `argc` is below 1.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Algorithm from Open-BFME-1's cos callback (donor); call shape read from the
/// BFME 2 body.
/// \see aptMathSin, aptMathTan


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathTan(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.tan(x)`: tangent of an angle in radians, as a float.
///
/// As aptMathSin, with the partial-tangent instruction (fptan). The body has no
/// guard for values where the tangent is undefined; the instruction's result is
/// boxed as it is.
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 1 is needed.
/// \return A new float value, or the undefined value when `argc` is below 1.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Algorithm from Open-BFME-1's tan callback (donor); call shape read from the
/// BFME 2 body.
/// \see aptMathSin, aptMathCos


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathAsin(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.asin(x)`: arc sine, in radians, as a float.
///
/// First argument converted to a float, widened to a double and passed to the C
/// library's `asin`; the result is boxed as a float value. The body has no
/// domain check, so an argument outside -1 to 1 gives whatever the C library
/// returns for it. Which entry point of the C runtime the call reaches is not
/// established here.
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 1 is needed.
/// \return A new float value, or the undefined value when `argc` is below 1.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Algorithm from Open-BFME-1's asin callback (donor); call shape read from the
/// BFME 2 body.
/// \see aptMathAcos, aptMathAtan


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathAcos(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.acos(x)`: arc cosine, in radians, as a float.
///
/// As aptMathAsin, with the C library's `acos`. An argument outside -1 to 1 is
/// not rejected by the body.
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 1 is needed.
/// \return A new float value, or the undefined value when `argc` is below 1.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Algorithm from Open-BFME-1's acos callback (donor); call shape read from the
/// BFME 2 body.
/// \see aptMathAsin, aptMathAtan


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathAtan(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.atan(x)`: arc tangent, in radians, as a float.
///
/// First argument converted to a float, widened to a double and passed to the C
/// library's `atan`; the result is boxed as a float value. For the
/// two-argument form that keeps the quadrant, use aptMathAtan2.
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 1 is needed.
/// \return A new float value, or the undefined value when `argc` is below 1.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Algorithm from Open-BFME-1's atan callback (donor); call shape read from the
/// BFME 2 body.
/// \see aptMathAtan2, aptMathAsin, aptMathAcos


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathAtan2(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.atan2(y, x)`: the angle of the point (x, y), in
///        radians, as a float.
///
/// The first script argument is y and the second is x, as in ActionScript and
/// the C library. Both are converted to floats and passed to the partial
/// arc-tangent instruction, which keeps the quadrant, so the result covers the
/// full circle (the one-argument aptMathAtan covers only half of it).
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 2 are needed.
/// \return A new float value, or the undefined value when `argc` is below 2.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Operation from Open-BFME-1's atan2 callback (donor). The BFME 2 body shows
/// which stack position feeds which operand of the instruction: the value
/// nearest the top of the stack is y.
/// \see aptMathAtan


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathExp(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.exp(x)`: e raised to x, as a float.
///
/// First argument converted to a float, widened to a double and passed to the C
/// library's `exp`; the result is boxed as a float value. The result for an
/// argument whose exponential lies outside the float range is whatever the
/// compiled code produces, narrowed to a float when boxed; it is not
/// established here.
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 1 is needed.
/// \return A new float value, or the undefined value when `argc` is below 1.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Algorithm from Open-BFME-1's exp callback (donor); call shape read from the
/// BFME 2 body.
/// \see aptMathLog, aptMathPow


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathLog(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.log(x)`: natural logarithm, as a float.
///
/// First argument converted to a float, widened to a double and passed to the C
/// library's `log`, the natural logarithm; the result is boxed as a float
/// value. There is no base-10 or base-2 variant among the Math names. The body
/// does not reject zero or negative arguments.
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 1 is needed.
/// \return A new float value, or the undefined value when `argc` is below 1.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Algorithm from Open-BFME-1's log callback (donor); call shape read from the
/// BFME 2 body.
/// \see aptMathExp, aptMathPow


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathPow(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.pow(base, exponent)`: a number raised to a power, as a
///        float.
///
/// Both arguments are converted to floats, the first being the base and the
/// second the exponent, and passed to the C runtime's power entry point
/// (_CIpow, reached through the executable's import thunk for msvcr71.dll). The
/// result is narrowed to a 32-bit float when it is boxed.
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 2 are needed.
/// \return A new float value, or the undefined value when `argc` is below 2.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Algorithm from Open-BFME-1's pow callback (donor). The two checked stack
/// reads, the numeric conversion and the runtime call are read from the BFME 2
/// body. The member lookup's construction of the callback's native-function
/// object ties it to `pow` independently of any name match with the donor.
/// \see aptMathExp, aptMathSqrt


/// \fn AptValue * aptMathSqrt(void *self, int argc)
/// \brief Native `Math.sqrt(x)`: square root, as a float.
///
/// First argument converted to a float and passed to the x87 square-root
/// instruction (fsqrt). The body does not reject a negative argument; the
/// instruction's result is boxed as it is.
///
/// \param self The context value callFunction passes. Not read.
/// \param argc Number of arguments the script passed. At least 1 is needed.
/// \return A new float value, or the undefined value when `argc` is below 1.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Algorithm from Open-BFME-1's sqrt callback (donor); the checked accessor and
/// the FPU instruction are read from the BFME 2 body.
/// \see aptMathPow


/// \class AptDate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_apt_math_date_natives
/// \brief The ActionScript `Date` object of the Apt interpreter: a local and a
///        UTC reading of the calendar plus a whole-hour time-zone offset.
///
/// A Date is a garbage-collected Apt value that a movie creates with
/// `new Date(...)`. It is allocated from Apt's pooled block allocator and, like
/// other object values, carries a property hash. The interpreter's
/// object-creation path, AptActionInterpreter::_createObject, passes up to
/// seven constructor arguments (year, month, date, hours, minutes, seconds and
/// the sub-second value) to the Date constructor, first script argument first.
/// Each argument is read as an integer, and every argument the script left out
/// is passed as -1. In the constructor -1 means "use the current local value",
/// so `new Date()` is the current time and an explicit -1 cannot be told apart
/// from an omitted argument. The values are stored as given: the year is not
/// shifted (a year of 99 stays 99) and nothing is range-checked.
///
/// \par State and invariants
/// - Two broken-down readings of the same instant, a local one and a UTC
///   one. Each holds second, minute, hour, day-of-month, month, year and a
///   sub-second field. Months run from 0 (January) to 11 and the day of the
///   month from 1.
///   The unit of the sub-second field is not established here. The
///   `...Milliseconds` methods read and write it, but the clock structure's
///   field is called hundredths in the debug database the layout comes from.
///   The clock structure also has a separate day field that no Date method
///   reads or writes.
/// - A time-zone offset in whole hours, local minus UTC. At construction the
///   Date calls the host clock function twice, stores the results as its local
///   and UTC readings and derives the offset from the hour difference, adding or subtracting 24 when the
///   day-of-month differs. Only the day-of-month and the hour are compared,
///   so minutes (half-hour zones) are lost, and a month or year boundary between
///   the two readings is not recognised.
/// - After the constructor overrides the local fields with any arguments it
///   was given, it calls AptDate::setDates to recompute the UTC reading from
///   the local one. The UTC clock the host returned is used only to derive the
///   offset.
/// - There is no epoch value. The two readings are the whole state, which is
///   why `getTime` and `setTime` below do not do what their names suggest.
///
/// \par The member names a movie can use
/// The member lookup of the Date object (the same lazy native-function cache
/// as for Math, one slot per name) serves exactly these 37 names, case
/// sensitively. A name in the wrong case logs a line starting `AptDate:
/// Incorrect case for` and quoting the name, raises an Apt assertion and gets
/// no member from this lookup, as for Math. The interpreter's own fallbacks
/// described for Math (the object's children, then the undefined-variable
/// callback) are not traced here for Date either.
///
/// | Names | Behaviour |
/// |---|---|
/// | `getDate`, `getMonth`, `getFullYear`, `getHours`, `getMinutes`, `getSeconds`, `getMilliseconds` | Return the stored field of the local reading as an integer. |
/// | `getYear` | The local year minus 1900. |
/// | `getDay` | The weekday, 0 for Sunday, computed from the local year, month and date (the weekday is not stored). |
/// | `getUTCDate`, `getUTCMonth`, `getUTCFullYear`, `getUTCHours`, `getUTCMinutes`, `getUTCSeconds`, `getUTCMilliseconds`, `getUTCDay` | The same for the UTC reading. |
/// | `getTimezoneOffset` | The absolute value of the offset hours times 60. The sign is dropped. |
/// | `getTime`, `setTime` | One shared stub that always returns the integer 0. `setTime` changes nothing. |
/// | `setDate`, `setMonth`, `setHours`, `setMinutes`, `setSeconds`, `setMilliseconds`, `setYear` | Store the first argument in the local reading, then recompute the UTC reading with AptDate::setDates. |
/// | `setFullYear` | Takes up to three arguments (year, then month, then date) into the local reading. |
/// | `setUTCDate`, `setUTCMonth`, `setUTCHours`, `setUTCMinutes`, `setUTCSeconds`, `setUTCMilliseconds`, `setUTCFullYear` | The same on the UTC reading, which then recomputes the local reading with the offset negated. |
/// | `toString` | Builds a string such as `Sun Jly 4 09:05:30 GMT+0100 2010`. |
/// | `UTC` | A stub callback that returns the undefined value. |
///
/// Every setter returns the integer 0, not the new time. A setter called with
/// no argument returns the undefined value and changes nothing. The setters
/// store their argument without range checks: `setMonth(14)` stores 14 in the
/// reading it was called on, and the other reading is rebuilt by
/// AptDate::setDates, which carries at most one day. `setYear` stores its
/// argument as the year unchanged although `getYear` subtracts 1900, so the
/// pair is not symmetric.
///
/// `toString` writes the weekday and month as three-letter names, the month of
/// July as `Jly`, the day of the month without padding, hours, minutes and
/// seconds in two digits, the literal `GMT`, a plus or minus sign (minus when
/// the offset is negative, otherwise plus), the absolute offset hours in two
/// digits, then `00` and a space, and the year in decimal. Unlike
/// `getTimezoneOffset`, which drops the sign, `toString` keeps it. As
/// reconstructed, the year is formatted into a
/// four-byte temporary, so a four-digit year writes its terminator one byte
/// past the temporary; the reconstruction keeps this on purpose.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - A Date is an ordinary garbage-collected value, held by whatever script
///   variable or stack slot refers to it.
/// - The native-function objects that the member lookup returns are not owned
///   by any Date. They are class-wide: one per name, built on first use, kept
///   alive by an extra reference and a collector root, and dropped together
///   by the static AptDate::CleanNativeFunctions when Apt shuts down.
/// - Each getter (all but the `getTime` stub) runs a checked cast of its
///   receiver first. Each setter does so after its argument-count check, so a
///   setter called with no argument never reaches the cast. The cast reports a
///   non-Date receiver through Apt's assertion hook (and breaks into the
///   debugger if Apt's break flag is set). It then returns the receiver anyway,
///   so execution does not stop at the cast.
///
/// \par Where it is used
/// - Created by AptActionInterpreter::_createObject for the name `Date`.
/// - Each `set...` method calls AptDate::setDates, and AptDate::setDates calls
///   AptDate::dateGetNumDaysInMonth when a carry crosses a month.
/// - Which shipped Apt movies use Date at all is not established here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Hard-coded: the native lookup serves exactly these 37 names,
///   case-sensitively, and returns no member for any other name; whether a
///   movie can store members on a Date value (the interpreter's variable
///   lookup also consults an object's own children) is not established here.
///   The stub behaviour is also fixed. On a Date value, `getTime` and
///   `setTime` are stubs and the `UTC` member returns the
///   undefined value; whether a static `Date.UTC` exists on the constructor was
///   not examined. The interpreter has a separate GetTimer action handler whose
///   behaviour is not covered here.
/// - Hard-coded: months from 0, years as given, `getYear` as year minus 1900,
///   and the one-day carry rule in AptDate::setDates.
/// - The readings come from whatever function the host installs in the clock
///   function pointer; if that is a wall clock, its readings can differ
///   between machines. Using such a reading in logic-side state, with each
///   machine consuming its own reading independently, would be a lockstep
///   risk (see \ref sub_gui_apt_modding). What the shipped game installs there
///   was not identified, and no shipped path that uses a Date reading is
///   established here.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The Date readings come from a host function pointer that takes the clock
///   structure to fill and an integer flag. The constructor body calls it
///   twice, with 1 for the reading it stores as local and 0 for the one it
///   stores as UTC, and has no null test, so a build must install a function
///   there before any movie constructs a Date. What the shipped game installs
///   there is not identified here.
/// - The offset is derived from the two readings, not queried. A zone with a
///   fraction of an hour is cut to whole hours, and two readings that straddle
///   a month boundary (for example 1 January local against 31 December UTC) give
///   a wrong offset.
/// - There is no 64-bit time value anywhere in the Date. Years are plain
///   integers, so there is no clock-wrap issue to port.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Names: the static callbacks (`sMethod_*`), AptDate::CleanNativeFunctions,
/// AptDate::setDates and AptDate::dateGetNumDaysInMonth match symbols in the
/// debug database of Apt version 0.19.03 from a console build of another EA
/// title. That is a strong name basis, but whether BFME 2 uses exactly that Apt
/// release is not established, and the debug database says nothing about
/// behaviour. The member list, the stubs, the argument handling, the format of
/// `toString` and the clock logic are read from the BFME 2 bodies and from the
/// BFME 2 member lookup. The layout of the clock structure is a donor fact
/// where the donor field names are used.
///
/// \see AptDate::setDates, AptDate::dateGetNumDaysInMonth,
///      AptDate::CleanNativeFunctions


/// \fn static void AptDate::CleanNativeFunctions()
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_apt_math_date_natives
/// \brief Releases the cached native-function objects behind all 37 Date
///        member names and empties the cache.
///
/// The Date member lookup builds one native-function object per name the first
/// time a script uses it and keeps the pointer in a class-wide cache slot, with
/// a reference of its own and a garbage-collection root mark. This function is
/// the counterpart for shutdown. For each of the 37 slots in a fixed order it
/// does the same thing: if the slot is not null, call the object's virtual
/// Release and set the slot to null.
///
/// It is safe to call twice, because empty slots are skipped. After it runs,
/// the next lookup of a name builds a fresh object, so the cache refills on
/// demand if Apt is brought up again.
///
/// \par Where it runs
/// Apt's teardown calls it right after the equivalent function for arrays. It
/// is reached from the Apt core shutdown routine, which the destructor of
/// AptPlayer (the game-side Apt player, \ref sub_gui_apt) reaches through a
/// host cleanup step. That step returns early, without calling the shutdown
/// routine, when a particular global pointer is null; what that pointer
/// designates is not established here. A second teardown helper repeats part
/// of the same release sequence and calls it as well. `tools/callers_of.py`
/// lists the callers.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Nothing a movie or data file can change: the slot list is fixed by the 37
/// names.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// A replacement Apt keeps this call (or its own equivalent) in its shutdown
/// path, otherwise the 37 objects stay referenced for the life of the process.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The name and the static, no-argument form come from the debug database of
/// Apt 0.19.03 from another EA title (donor, strong). That each slot holds the
/// object for the member of the same name, and that cleanup is a release-and-
/// null per slot, are read from BFME 2: the cleanup walks the same slots that
/// the Date member lookup fills.
/// \see AptDate


/// \fn void AptDate::setDates(AptSysClock *source, AptSysClock *destination, int offset)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_apt_math_date_natives
/// \brief Rebuilds one of a Date's two clock readings from the other by
///        shifting the hour by a whole-hour offset.
///
/// A Date keeps a local reading and a UTC reading. Every mutator rewrites one
/// field of one reading and then calls this function to regenerate the other.
/// The local setters pass the local reading as `source`, the UTC reading as
/// `destination` and the Date's time-zone hours as `offset`. The UTC setters
/// swap the two readings and negate the offset. The Date constructor makes the
/// same call, local to UTC, after it has applied its arguments.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Year, month and day of the month are copied from `source`. The
///   destination hour is the source hour minus `offset`.
/// - If the new hour is above 23, the destination hour is reduced modulo 24
///   and the date moves forward one day. When the date passes the month length
///   (from AptDate::dateGetNumDaysInMonth) it becomes 1 and the month advances;
///   after month 11 the month wraps to 0 and the year advances.
/// - If the new hour is below 0, the destination hour is set to 24 minus
///   `offset`, the date moves back one day, and when it drops below 1 the month
///   steps back (month 0 wraps to 11 and the year goes back) and the date
///   becomes the length of that month.
/// - Minute, second and the sub-second field are copied unchanged. Time zones
///   with a fraction of an hour are therefore not represented. The clock's
///   separate day field is not copied or changed.
///
/// \warning Two retail quirks are kept on purpose. In the below-zero branch the
///          new hour is 24 minus the offset, not 24 plus the source hour
///          minus the offset, so it is right only when the source hour is 0:
///          a local reading of 02:30 with offset 5 comes out as hour 19 on the
///          previous day, where plain arithmetic gives 21. And the date moves by
///          at most one day, so an offset of 24 hours or more is not fully
///          normalised.
///
/// \param source      The reading to convert from. Not modified.
/// \param destination [out] The reading to overwrite. Callers always pass the
///                    Date's other reading, never the same object as `source`.
/// \param offset      Hours subtracted from the source hour. A Date passes its
///                    local-minus-UTC hours to go from local to UTC and the
///                    negated value to go back.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Name and signature from the debug database of Apt 0.19.03 from another EA
/// title (donor, strong). The conversion, including both quirks above, is read
/// from the BFME 2 body, and the retail setter and constructor callers fix the
/// meaning of the three arguments. The field names of the clock structure,
/// and its day and sub-second fields, are donor names.
/// \see AptDate, AptDate::dateGetNumDaysInMonth


/// \fn int AptDate::dateGetNumDaysInMonth(int month, int year)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_apt_math_date_natives
/// \brief Returns how many days a month has, counting February's extra day in
///        leap years.
///
/// AptDate::setDates uses it to decide whether a one-day carry crosses into
/// the next month, and to find the last day of the previous month when it
/// carries backward. It does not read the Date it is called on.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// `month` counts from 0 for January. Months 0, 2, 4, 6, 7, 9 and 11 have 31
/// days and months 3, 5, 8 and 10 have 30. Month 1 has 28 days plus 1 when
/// the year is a leap year, using the Gregorian rule (every fourth year, except
/// centuries, except every fourth century), through the class's leap-year
/// helper. Any other month number reports an assertion through Apt's assert
/// hook (the retail assertion names the source file AptDate.cpp), executes a
/// debugger break when Apt's break flag is set, and returns 31 anyway.
///
/// \param month Month number from 0 (January) to 11 (December).
/// \param year  The full year, used only to decide February in a leap year.
/// \return The number of days, 28 to 31.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Name and signature from the debug database of Apt 0.19.03 from another EA
/// title (donor, strong); the assertion and the month switch are read from the
/// BFME 2 body.
/// \see AptDate::setDates


/// \class AptValue
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_apt_math_date_natives
/// \brief The base of every ActionScript value in the Apt library: numbers,
///        strings, booleans, objects, arrays, dates, movie-clip instances and
///        functions.
///
/// Apt scripts manipulate values through this one class: the interpreter's
/// value stack is a stack of value pointers (\ref sub_gui_apt_runtime), and
/// native callbacks such as the Math and Date methods take and return AptValue
/// pointers.
///
/// \par Lifetime
/// - Values are reference counted, through virtual AddRef and Release calls, and
///   are also tracked by Apt's garbage collector. Code that caches a value for
///   a long time, as the Date member lookup does for its native-function
///   objects, takes its own reference and marks the value as a collector
///   root. Apt's shutdown releases those caches explicitly.
/// - Integers, floats and booleans come from per-kind pools that Apt clears at
///   shutdown, and string values are recycled through a free list.
/// - The undefined value is a single global object. Native callbacks return it
///   when a call has too few arguments, and several conversions compare
///   against it by address.
///
/// \par Kinds
/// A type number held in the value's flag word distinguishes the kinds. Kinds
/// that the code documented here depends on: integer, float, boolean, string
/// (two type numbers are treated as strings), and Date. Predicates such as
/// "is integer" or "is string" read that number and also require the value to be
/// defined. The checked casts (for example the Date cast used by the Date
/// getters, setters and `toString` callback) report through Apt's assertion
/// hook when the kind does not match, and then return the value anyway.
///
/// \par Strings
/// A string value keeps its text in an EAStringC, a reference-counted string
/// with shared buffers. AptValue::SetString is the way the engine puts a C string
/// into such a value.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The scalar kinds and the conversion rules between them are hard-coded
/// (see the file overview of AptMathCallbacksBFME2.cpp for the number
/// conversion). Which values a movie creates and passes is data in the movie.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// A native-function object stores its callback address in a 32-bit integer.
/// That is one place where a 64-bit port has to change the value model and not
/// only the callbacks.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// That the first two virtual slots are an add-reference call and a release
/// call, and that a value carries a collector-root count that callers set, are
/// read from BFME 2 bodies: the Date cleanup (Release), the construction of the
/// native-function cache entries (collector-root marking and AddRef) and the
/// collector-root setter. The names AddRef and Release are not BFME 2 evidence:
/// they are donor debug-database slot names (from the Apt 0.19.03 database
/// described for AptDate) that the ledger records. That values come in the
/// kinds above is read from BFME 2 bodies.
/// Some source units declare this class under a stand-in name; search by role
/// (\ref page_reading_code).
///
/// \see AptValue::SetString, AptDate


/// \fn void AptValue::SetString(const char *text)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_apt_math_date_natives
/// \brief Replaces the text of a string value with a copy of a C string.
///
/// This is how the interpreter and the native objects fill in the string
/// values they hand back to a script. The engine creates a string value (the
/// string factory, AptString::Create), then calls this with the text.
///
/// \par Where it is used
/// The callers include the typeof handler, which sets the text for each kind
/// it names; the to-string and Trace handlers, which reach it only when the
/// operand is undefined and a global Apt version value equals 7; the
/// string-addition handlers, which build the replacement text for an undefined
/// operand behind the same version check (the dedicated string-add handler
/// builds it whenever the check passes, the Add2 handler only for an undefined
/// operand beside a string operand); the Date `toString` callback, the string `split` pieces, the
/// text-format font name, the Error object's `message` and `name`, XML node
/// strings, the pooled string factory itself, and AptSetInternalVariable.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - It builds a temporary EAStringC from `text`. The EAStringC constructor
///   allocates a buffer and copies the text, except that an empty string shares
///   the single empty-string buffer.
/// - It assigns the temporary to the value's string member. EAStringC assignment
///   takes a reference to the new buffer before releasing the old one, so the
///   previous text is freed when nothing else shares it.
/// - The temporary is destroyed on the way out and drops its own reference,
///   leaving the buffer owned by the value alone. The function keeps no pointer
///   into `text`, so the caller's string may be freed immediately.
///
/// \param text The new text. It must not be null: EAStringC reports an
///             assertion for a null pointer through Apt's hook, and then reads
///             the pointer anyway.
///
/// \warning The function does not check the kind of the value. Every caller
///          read for this page passes a value made by the string factory
///          (AptString::Create) or by a path that calls it, but not every
///          caller in the binary was examined. What happens when it is called
///          on a value of another kind is not established here.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// EAStringC reads its length through a 16-bit field in retail, which suggests
/// a limit on string length. The exact limit is not established here.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The body is read from BFME 2: a temporary, an assignment, a destructor. That
/// the target of the assignment is the value's string member is proven by the
/// assignment call; only the position of that member is established, not the
/// layout of the rest of the object. The name and the AptValue spelling are the
/// ledger's; which class owns this member in the original source is not
/// established, since callers cast string values to AptValue to reach it. The
/// set of callers above is read from the reconstructed callers and is not
/// complete.
/// \see AptValue, AptDate
